schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Proof of Work vs Proof of Stake
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    The two dominant consensus mechanisms blockchains use to agree on the
    record book, with very different energy footprints — the article uses this
    distinction to give a precise (not hand-wavy) answer to "is crypto bad for
    the environment."
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Used in common-concerns.md to explain why Bitcoin (proof of work,
    ~140-175 TWh/year per Cambridge CBECI) and Ethereum/Celo (proof of stake,
    ~99.95% less energy after Ethereum's 2022 "Merge") cannot be answered with
    one blanket environmental verdict.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - mining (the proof-of-work compute race)
    - staking (the proof-of-stake deposit-and-follow-rules model)
  important_distinctions:
    - "the network/consensus method, not 'crypto' as a category, determines energy footprint"
  tensions:
    - security-via-energy-expenditure (PoW) vs security-via-economic-stake (PoS)
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating "crypto" as a single monolithic energy story erases the real,
    large difference between individual networks' designs.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/common-concerns.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-1fd434ce7666
  source_lineage: common-concerns.md
